    title: "Reading Passage",
    level: "C1",
    text: "Prepositions of place are among the most challenging aspects of English for advanced learners, because their usage often defies logical translation. The basic trio — 'in', 'on', and 'at' — follow patterns that must be learned rather than reasoned out. We say 'in a city', 'in a country', 'in a room', and 'in a car', but 'on a bus', 'on a train', and 'on a plane'. We are 'at the station', 'at the office', 'at school', and 'at home' — but 'in hospital' (British) or 'in the hospital' (American). Beyond these basics, English has a rich set of compound prepositions: 'in front of' (opposite of behind), 'next to' or 'beside' (adjacent), 'between' (with two things on each side), 'among' (surrounded by multiple things), 'opposite' (facing directly), 'beneath' (under, often formal or literary), and 'beyond' (further than, past). Movement prepositions add another layer: 'through' (entering one side and exiting the other), 'across' (from one side to the other of a surface), 'along' (following the length of), 'past' (moving by without stopping), 'towards' (in the direction of), and 'around' (circling or surrounding). Many prepositions also carry metaphorical meanings: 'beyond comprehension', 'beneath contempt', 'above suspicion', 'under pressure', and 'between jobs'. These figurative uses are what truly mark C1-level mastery.",
